From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 06:47:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 063/119] scene-referred S1: publish the camera's pre-exposure
 and latch it once per render view

First item of Stage S1. It builds the road the exposure travels on - bus parameter, render-thread
latch, the five m_fAdaptedSceneScaleLBuffer sites - and deliberately drives 1.0 down it, so the
picture is byte-identical to S0. Item 2 puts the real number in and drops the tone map's own
ComputeExposure() in the same commit, because changing either one alone is a frame of double (or
zero) exposure.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s1-exposure-publication.md, approach A (the post-effect bus).
Research: scene-notes/research/s1-adapted-scene-scale.md sections 1, 2 and 6.

- Global_User_SceneExposure, an AddParamFloatNoTransition next to S0's Global_User_SceneReferred.
  Default and reset value 1.0f, not 0.0f: a missing publication has to be a no-op. Resolved once
  at Init() and read through CPostEffectsMgr::GetSceneExposureParam(), the same shape S0's review
  gave the request, and nulled in Release() with it. No transition, because blending two exposures
  on the way to a new value is a fade the camera never asked for.

- Approach A verified rather than assumed, which was the open item in the decision note.
  CParamFloat holds one value per command buffer (m_threadSafeData[RT_COMMAND_BUF_COUNT]) and
  CParamFloat::SyncMainWithRender() copies fill -> process at the frame boundary. So the value the
  render thread reads is the one ccam published for that frame and it cannot move under a frame in
  flight. Approach B (E3DPARAM + S3DEngineCommon) was not needed.

- The latch is CD3D9Renderer::LatchSceneReferredExposure(), called from RT_RenderScene() at the
  point where the engine already resets m_fAdaptedSceneScaleLBuffer to 1.0f, immediately before
  the graphics-pipeline scope - so it precedes CGraphicsPipeline::Update() (which builds the
  per-view constant buffer that item 3 will use) and Execute() (which builds the light lists).
  It writes m_fSceneReferredExposure[GetRenderThreadID()] and assigns
  m_fAdaptedSceneScaleLBuffer from it. Off the switch it writes the same 1.0f the line above it
  just wrote, so the stock path is unchanged; the five consumer sites are not edited at all.
  A per-view latch, not a per-frame one, is also what stereo and the editor's extra viewports
  need: one value for the whole view, no live bus read from inside a stage. That is the hazard the
  research note documents - the sun and the tiled lights are packed in CTiledLightVolumesStage
  while the fog lights are packed in CVolumetricFogStage, and a scalar that is the whole image's
  brightness must not differ between them.

- Probes: decisions/s1-probe-exposure.md approach A is implemented here rather than left to S2,
  because it is one line at the latch. SHDF_CUBEMAPGEN forces the exposure to 1.0, so cube maps
  always store unexposed radiance in engine units and the tint multiply the hook already performs
  exposes them exactly once at sample time. Without it the factor would be squared, and BC6UH is
  unsigned so nothing could carry a compensation back in. This supersedes spec D3's "exclude
  probes from the hook" wording; the spec text is amended in item 5.

- Published values are validated at the latch (finite, positive, clamped to [1e-12, 1e12]): a zero
  scale would black the frame out with no error anywhere and an infinity would poison the
  luminance measure. No reachable EV100 in [-6, 24] comes near either end.

- ccam gains ApplySceneExposure() / RestoreSceneExposure() and
  ComputeSceneReferredExposureScale(), wired into ENTITY_EVENT_UPDATE and
  ENTITY_EVENT_COMPONENT_PROPERTY_CHANGED next to ApplySceneReferred() (editor-live: with the
  scene-referred path on, the exposure is the picture), and into RestoreAllEffects() and
  ClearSavedBaselines(). ComputeSceneReferredExposureScale() returns 1.0f in this commit.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): CryRenderD3D11, CinematicCamera.

